hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"20623","Yazid narrated to us, Kahmas ibn al-Hasan informed us, Abd Allah ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, a man from Anazah called Za'idah or Mazidah ibn Hawalah narrated to me, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on one of his journeys. The people alighted at a stop and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) alighted in the shade of a lote tree. He saw me coming from attending to a need of mine, with none but him and his scribe. He said: Shall we write you down, O Ibn Hawalah? I said: For what, O Messenger of Allah? He said: He turned away from me and faced the scribe. He said: Then I drew near less than that. He said: He said: Shall we write you down, O Ibn Hawalah? I said: For what, O Messenger of Allah? He said: He turned away from me and faced the scribe. He said: Then I came and stood over them, and there at the top of the writing were Abu Bakr and Umar. I thought they would only be written in good. He said: Shall we write you down, O Ibn Hawalah? I said: Yes, O Prophet of Allah. He said: O Ibn Hawalah, what will you do in a fitnah that will rage in the regions of the earth as if it were bovine horns? He said: I said: What shall I do, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Adhere to Sham. Then he said: What will you do in a fitnah as if the first of it were the leap of a rabbit? He said: I do not know how he said it in the latter, and that I had known how he said it in the latter would be dearer to me than such-and-such."
